        "title": "Part XV. Prince's",
        "content": "<p>Prince's.</p><p>MS. ' Huns ,' but Z. reads ' Hugs .' Cf. W.</p><p>'O'er land of Burgundians,' Gn.</p><p>Z. has no point, W. puts (;), Gn. (.)</p><p>'Hurried,' Z. 3</p><p>'He,' W.</p><p>'Which,' Z.</p><p>'In the terrible danger,' Gn.</p><p>Lit. 'in like manner,' adv.</p><p>Add 'at the work.'</p><p>'Diminished,' Gn.</p><p>i.e. , with precious stones. Kr. reads '(rattled strong shields).'</p><p>'Gold,' Kr. 'Lord of the house,' Gn. Cf. W.</p><p>The Bishop of Rome.</p><p>Lit., 'smiths of lore.'</p><p>Z. supposes lacuna of one verse; W. thinks it unnecessary.</p><p>Lit., 'the woman.'</p><p>Lit., 'to the sea,' or 'sea-journey.'</p><p>A.-S. form retained for the sake of the accent and alliteration.</p><p>Lit., 'kinswoman.' The Elizabethan 'Kesar' would preserve the alliteration in this line.</p><p>Gn. and Z. W. omits.</p><p>Psalms xvi. 8, 9.</p><p>Isaiah i. 2, 3.</p><p>Gn., Z., W.</p><p>So W. 'Wrongs have committed,' Gm., Gn. and Z. [?]</p><p>W.</p><p>Add 'after that.'</p><p>Lacuna in MS., emended by Gn.</p><p>i.e. , 'defence, protector.'</p><p>Lit., 'under the lap (or bosom) of sins.'</p><p>MS. rex (Latin?), Z.; 'oppression of care' ( cearces ), Gn.; 'of hunger' ( ceaces ), Gm.; 'of smoke' ( rêces ), Schubert; rex = cyninges , Sievers and W.</p><p>Z.</p><p>Or, 'war,' Gn.; 'further oft,' Gm.</p><p>No lacuna in MS. Gn. 1 inserted one line, but Gn. 2 one word ( fêonda ), which W. prefers. Text as Z. ( fêondes ), which Sievers approves.</p><p>'Mindful,' Gm. and Gn.; 'suffering,' Z. [?].</p><p>Referring to the sword.</p><p>Gn., or 'foul,' Z.</p><p>Lit., 'joy-wood.'</p><p>Lit., 'knee.'</p><p>So Z.; 'rebellion for this,' W. See W.'s note.</p><p>Julian the Apostate, suggests Gn.</p><p>'That,' relative, though it may be taken as conjunction, as Z.</p><p>So Z.; 'The noble wood,' Gm. and Gn.</p><p>Latin, rex .</p><p>So Z.; 'With pomp of array,' Gn.</p><p>Lit., 'coming.'</p><p>Same expression as in 1054.</p><p>Lit., 'will-giver,' i.e. , the queen.</p><p>i.e. , her ornaments of gold.</p><p>Gn.'s emendation.</p><p>Here properly ends the legend of the Finding of the Cross. The last canto contains reflections of the poet.</p><p>Gn.'s emendation.</p><p>Lit.,'appled gold.'</p><p>The words in italics are the names of the runes that make up the name CYNEWULF. This artificial use of words makes the interpretation obscure, and scholars differ about it.</p><p>Or, 'those who.'</p><p>Gn., Z.</p><p>JUDITH.</p>",
